art:artigo_N18_3011_trabalho_futuro_1 a art:TrabalhoFuturo ;
    art:extraidoDe art:artigo_N18_3011_secao_7 ;
    art:pontuacao 7 ;
    art:texto "Three research directions follow from this work and other similar projects, e.g., Hahn-Powell et al. (2017); Wu et al. (2014): i) improving quality and enriching content of the literature graph (e.g., ontology matching and knowledge base population). ii) aggregating domain-specific extractions across many papers to enable a better understanding of the literature as a whole (e.g., identifying demographic biases in clinical trial participants and summarizing empirical results on important tasks). iii) exploring the literature via natural language interfaces."@en .

art:artigo_P07_1088_contribuicao_1 a art:Contribuicao ;
    art:extraidoDe art:artigo_P07_1088_secao_2 ;
    art:pontuacao 6 ;
    art:texto "The paper introduces the insight that the subfield of language modeling provides unsupervised methods that can be leveraged to assess sparse extractions."@en .

art:artigo_P07_1088_contribuicao_2 a art:Contribuicao ;
    art:extraidoDe art:artigo_P07_1088_secao_2 ;
    art:pontuacao 6 ;
    art:texto "The paper introduces an HMM-based technique for checking whether two arguments are of the proper type for a relation."@en .

art:artigo_P07_1088_contribuicao_3 a art:Contribuicao ;
    art:extraidoDe art:artigo_P07_1088_secao_2 ;
    art:pontuacao 6 ;
    art:texto "The paper introduces a relational n-gram model for the purpose of determining whether a sentence that mentions multiple arguments actually expresses a particular relationship between them."@en .

art:artigo_P07_1088_contribuicao_4 a art:Contribuicao ;
    art:extraidoDe art:artigo_P07_1088_secao_2 ;
    art:pontuacao 6 ;
    art:texto "The paper introduces a novel languagemodeling system called REALM that combines both HMM-based models and relational n-gram models, and shows that REALM reduces error by an average of 39% over previous methods, when applied to sparse extraction data."@en .

art:artigo_P07_1088_metodo_1 a art:Metodo ;
    art:extraidoDe art:artigo_P07_1088_secao_1 ;
    art:pontuacao 3 ;
    art:texto "Because REALM pre-computes language models based on its corpus and does not require any hand-tagged seeds, it is far more scalable than approaches that learn models for each individual relation from hand-tagged data."@en .

art:artigo_P07_1088_metodo_2 a art:Metodo ;
    art:extraidoDe art:artigo_P07_1088_secao_2 ;
    art:pontuacao 4 ;
    art:texto "Second, we employ an instance of the distributional hypothesis (Harris, 1985), which can be phrased as follows: different instances of the same semantic relation tend to appear in similar textual contexts."@en .

art:artigo_P07_1088_metodo_3 a art:Metodo ;
    art:extraidoDe art:artigo_P07_1088_secao_2 ;
    art:pontuacao 5 ;
    art:texto "We assess sparse extractions by comparing the contexts in which they appear to those of more common extractions."@en .

art:artigo_P07_1088_objetivo_1 a art:Objetivo ;
    art:extraidoDe art:artigo_P07_1088_secao_1 ;
    art:pontuacao 4 ;
    art:texto "This paper shows how to assess the correctness of sparse extractions by utilizing unsupervised language models."@en .

art:artigo_P07_1088_objetivo_2 a art:Objetivo ;
    art:extraidoDe art:artigo_P07_1088_secao_2 ;
    art:pontuacao 4 ;
    art:texto "This paper introduces the idea that language modeling techniques such as n-gram statistics (Manning and Sch ̈utze, 1999) and HMMs (Rabiner, 1989) can be used to effectively assess sparse extractions."@en .

art:artigo_P07_1088_problema_1 a art:Problema ;
    art:extraidoDe art:artigo_P07_1088_secao_2 ;
    art:pontuacao 5 ;
    art:texto "However, Zipf's Law governs the distribution of extractions."@en .

art:artigo_P07_1088_problema_2 a art:Problema ;
    art:extraidoDe art:artigo_P07_1088_secao_2 ;
    art:pontuacao 5 ;
    art:texto "This problem is particularly acute when moving beyond unary relations."@en .

